WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.650
SPEAKER: When it
comes to the cloud,

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:03.990
there's more than one
way to serve a file.

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:06.210
In this episode of
Cloud Storage Bytes,

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:09.210
we walk you through all the ways
to serve data from the cloud

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:11.745
so you can determine what the
best fit is for your needs.

00:00:11.745 --> 00:00:13.928
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:22.360
You can use cloud storage to
serve assets to your customers.

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:24.215
And depending on
your application,

00:00:24.215 --> 00:00:26.590
you might need different
methods of getting that data out

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:28.255
of a cloud storage bucket.

00:00:28.255 --> 00:00:32.320
You know you've got options, so
let's go ahead and take a look.

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:34.600
First, we've got
client libraries.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.580
If you need to download
assets directly

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:38.680
to your existing
application, this

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:40.480
is something for you to explore.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:43.570
And trust me when I say
we speak your language.

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:48.025
From C++ to Ruby, check out the
linked documentation for more.

00:00:48.025 --> 00:00:51.640
And up next you have
the gsutil application.

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:53.770
Gsutil is a Python
application that

00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:57.430
lets you access cloud storage
from the command line.

00:00:57.430 --> 00:00:59.950
And, for example,
running this command

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:02.020
will download from a
cloud storage bucket

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:04.550
to a local path on your device.

00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:07.210
And like any
awesome tool, gsutil

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:09.430
comes with a plethora
of options to configure

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:12.430
to your specific use
case, like the dash m

00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:16.710
command, which allows for copy
options to occur in parallel.

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:19.290
You can also go directly
to the REST APIs

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:22.740
using other programs like Curl
to fetch your files directly

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:25.930
and allowing the user
to log in with oauth.

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:28.810
And, finally, you can
always access your objects

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:30.520
right from the cloud console.

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:33.010
Simply select the
desired bucket or object,

00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:35.990
click Save As in the
handy dropdown menu.

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:38.260
This is a great way to
grab individual files

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:41.790
when you're debugging or
going through things manually.

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:44.220
Retrieving files is
really just the start

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:46.780
as there's so much more you
can do with cloud storage,

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:49.140
but that's for another episode.

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:51.397
Subscribe, give
us a like, and let

00:01:51.397 --> 00:01:52.980
us know what features
of cloud storage

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:54.690
you'd like to learn more about.

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:57.180
Thanks for joining us for this
quick bite of cloud storage.

00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:59.930
[MUSIC PLAYING]

